{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bc3f39b528e2f7b6", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tIdentity-Faithful Audio-Visual Target Speaker Extraction with Q IANG DA\n2\tand VOX B LINK 2-AVSE\n3\tPeijun Yang\n4\t1\n5\t\n6\t, Zhan Jin\n7\t\n8\t3\n9\t\n10\t, Juan Liu\n11\t\n12\t2,3,*\n13\t\n14\t, Ming Li\n15\t\n16\t2,4,*\n17\t\n18\tSchool of Cyber Science and Engineering, Wuhan University, Wuhan, China\n19\t2\n20\t\n21\tSchool of Artificial Intelligence, Wuhan University, Wuhan, China\n22\t3\n23\t\n24\t4\n25\t\n26\t1,2\n27\t\n28\tSchool of Computer Science, Wuhan University, Wuhan, China\n29\t\n30\tSchool of Artificial Intelligence, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shenzhen, China\n31\tliujuan@whu.edu.cn, ming.li.cuhksz@gmail.com\n32\t\n33\tarXiv:2608.03964v1 [eess.AS] 4 Aug 2026\n34\t\n35\tAbstract\n36\tAudio-visual target speaker extraction should\n37\treturn the speaker indicated by the video, yet\n38\ta separator can ignore the visual cue and\n39\trepeatedly output the acoustically dominant\n40\tvoice. We introduce Q IANG DA, a Mandarin\n41\tAV-TSE benchmark of jointly recorded real\n42\ttwo-speaker mixtures with synchronized multiview video. Each scene also contains preceding A-only and B-only stages that provide\n43\tin-scene speaker references. It contains 77\n44\tscenes and 7,598 clips (11.84 hours), including\n45\t6,042 dual-annotated mixtures. After processing there leave 6,038 evaluable mixtures and\n46\t12,076 target-speaker rows. We additionally\n47\tcurate VOX B LINK 2-AVSE from VoxBlink2,\n48\tcomprising 250,828 synchronized audio–lipROI pairs from 28,421 identities and 766.17\n49\thours of speech. Our extractor uses frozen,\n50\t1,280-dimensional projected AV-HuBERT features, target-conditioned training, and layerwise feature modulation. We jointly evaluate\n51\tcontent with Qwen3-ASR-1.7B CER and target\n52\tidentity with WeSpeaker ResNet34 plus Overlapped Speech Detection (OSD). On the complete manifest, the best archived checkpoint obtains 0.2261 CER, 82.22% strict output correctness, and 69.53% both-output strict success.\n53\t\n54\t1\n55\t\n56\tIntroduction\n57\t\n58\tTarget speaker extraction (TSE) estimates one desired speaker from a mixture by conditioning a\n59\tseparator on a target cue. Audio-only systems\n60\tcommonly use a pre-recorded enrollment utterance\n61\t(Žmolíková et al., 2019; Zeng and Li, 2025). Video\n62\tprovides a naturally synchronized alternative: visible lip motion identifies when a person speaks and\n63\t*\n64\t\n65\tindicates the corresponding author.\n66\t\n67\tsupplies phonetic evidence that remains useful during voice overlap (Ochiai et al., 2019; Pan et al.,\n68\t2022). Audio-visual TSE is therefore attractive for\n69\tmeetings, interviews, and human–machine interaction.\n70\tProgress is nevertheless difficult to measure with\n71\tmixtures created from independently recorded utterances. Such mixtures provide isolated sources\n72\tfor signal-reconstruction metrics, but they do not\n73\treproduce a shared physical scene in which target speech, interference, reverberation, and background noise are captured together. Real mixtures\n74\texpose this synthetic-to-real gap, but usually lack\n75\tisolated clean overlap sources for intrusive evaluation (Li et al., 2025). Moreover, a separator may\n76\tfavor one voice and weakly use its visual input.\n77\tEvaluating each visual cue independently can miss\n78\tthis failure: two plausible-sounding outputs may\n79\tboth belong to the same person.\n80\tWe introduce Q IANG DA (“QiangDa” denotes\n81\ta quick-response or buzz-in interaction) to\n82\tbenchmark identity-faithful AV-TSE on purposerecorded real mixtures with synchronized audio\n83\tand multi-view video. Every A+B mixture is\n84\tqueried with both visual targets, while preceding\n85\tA-only and B-only stages provide scene-matched\n86\tvoice references. Speaker similarity and CER\n87\tthen evaluate requested identity and linguistic content without requiring isolated clean waveforms\n88\tfor the overlap. We complement this benchmark\n89\twith VOX B LINK 2-AVSE, a large identity-disjoint\n90\taudio-lip corpus curated for AVSE training, and\n91\twith an AV-HuBERT-conditioned TF-GridNet.\n92\tOur contributions are threefold:\n93\t• We release Q IANG DA, a controlled real-scene\n94\ttest set benchmark with jointly recorded overlap, scene-matched solo references, multiple\n95\t\n96\t\fdistances, and three synchronized viewpoints.\n97\t• We release VOX B LINK 2-AVSE, containing\n98\t250,828 paired utterances from 28,421 identities and 766.17 hours, with disjoint training\n99\tand development speakers.\n100\t• We develop an AV-HuBERT-conditioned\n101\taudio-visual extractor and ablate its training\n102\tdata, layer-wise FiLM, GridNet capacity, and\n103\tauxiliary speaker-margin loss using both identity and content metrics.\n104\t\n105\t2\n106\t\n107\tRelated Work\n108\t\n109\t2.1\n110\t\n111\tSpeech Separation and Target Extraction\n112\t\n113\tConv-TasNet established a strong time-domain\n114\tspeech-separation baseline (Luo and Mesgarani,\n115\t2019), while TF-GridNet combines frequency and\n116\ttemporal modeling in the time-frequency domain\n117\t(Wang et al., 2023). Unlike permutation-invariant\n118\tseparation, TSE uses an auxiliary cue to specify which source to extract. SpeakerBeam uses\n119\tan enrollment utterance (Žmolíková et al., 2019),\n120\twhereas Multimodal SpeakerBeam incorporates\n121\taudio and visual clues (Ochiai et al., 2019). VisualVoice imposes cross-modal consistency (Gao\n122\tand Grauman, 2021), and USEV addresses mixtures ranging from no overlap to complete overlap\n123\t(Pan et al., 2022). AV-GridNet further grounds TFGridNet with a target face recording (Pan et al.,\n124\t2023). Our focus is complementary: we benchmark AV-TSE on jointly recorded real mixtures\n125\tand test whether changing the visual target changes\n126\tthe extracted identity, rather than only whether one\n127\tprompted output sounds clean.\n128\t2.2\n129\t\n130\tVisual Speech Representations and\n131\tCorpora\n132\t\n133\tAV-HuBERT learns audio-visual speech representations through masked multimodal cluster prediction (Shi et al., 2022). M2S-AVSR extends this line\n134\ttoward multi-view and real-scene robustness (Su\n135\tet al., 2026). We use the visual-only AV-HuBERT\n136\tbranch and the learned linear projection from the\n137\tlatter implementation, rather than its ASR decoder.\n138\tVoxCeleb and VoxBlink2 collect large-scale inthe-wild face–voice material for speaker recognition (Nagrani et al., 2017; Lin et al., 2024), LRS2\n139\tand LRS3 supply sentence-level material for visual\n140\tspeech recognition (Chung et al., 2017; Afouras\n141\tet al., 2018); and AVSpeech enables audio-visual\n142\t\n143\tmixtures at scale (Ephrat et al., 2018). These corpora provide valuable pretraining material, but they\n144\tdo not directly benchmark AV-TSE on jointly captured overlap, where target speech, interference,\n145\treverberation, and background noise are recorded\n146\tthrough the same physical scene. REAL-T exposes\n147\tthe corresponding synthetic-to-real gap for audioonly TSE by constructing test mixtures from conversational recordings and evaluating ASR error\n148\tbecause isolated clean sources are unavailable (Li\n149\tet al., 2025). Q IANG DA targets this gap for AVTSE with synchronized audio, multi-view video,\n150\tand real two-speaker mixtures. Its A-only and Bonly stages provide in-scene enrollment references;\n151\tthe benchmark centers on extracting either speaker\n152\tfrom the same recorded A+B mixture and evaluates the outputs with speaker similarity and CER,\n153\twithout requiring isolated clean waveforms for the\n154\toverlap. VOX B LINK 2-AVSE complements this\n155\treal-mixture benchmark with quality-controlled,\n156\taligned audio-lip pairs for AVTSE training.\n157\t\n158\t3\n159\t\n160\tThe Q IANG DA Benchmark\n161\t\n162\t3.1\n163\t\n164\tTask Definition and Recording Protocol\n165\t\n166\tLet an overlap recording be x(t) = sA (t)+sB (t)+\n167\tn(t), where n includes room acoustics and background noise. Given the visual stream vq for target\n168\tq ∈ {A, B}, the extractor predicts\n169\tŝq = fθ (x, vq ).\n170\t\n171\t(1)\n172\t\n173\tThe desired paired behavior is fθ (x, vA ) ≈ sA and\n174\tfθ (x, vB ) ≈ sB . This requirement rules out visual\n175\tbypass, in which both prompts return the same\n176\tdominant source.\n177\tEach scene records two stationary participants\n178\tin a shared environment. Stage I contains A-only\n179\tspeech, Stage II contains B-only speech, and Stage\n180\tIII contains simultaneous reading. The first two\n181\tstages establish in-scene references for voice, face,\n182\tillumination, camera position, and acoustics. In\n183\tStage III, one participant reads continuously while\n184\tthe other chooses when to start, producing sustained but naturally timed overlap. The recording\n185\tconditions remain fixed within a scene. The corpus\n186\tstudied here contains read Mandarin speech only.\n187\tNon-verbal vocalizations are excluded. Audio from\n188\tthe headset and near-field microphones is recorded\n189\tat 48 kHz, whereas the camera-synchronized microphones record at 16 kHz.\n190\t\n191\t\fFigure 1: Q IANG DA recording setup and a representative frontal-view frame. Panel (a) shows the camera geometry\n192\tand microphone arrangement; panel (b) shows a 0.5-m example with the two speaker regions marked by dashed\n193\tboxes.\n194\t\n195\t3.2\n196\t\n197\tGeometry and Acquisition\n198\t\n199\tFigure 1 summarizes the recording geometry and\n200\tshows a representative frontal-view frame. The\n201\tprotocol uses distances of 0.5, 1.5, and 3 m and\n202\tside-view angles of 30◦ , 45◦ , and 60◦ . Each scene\n203\thas one central camera and two cameras placed\n204\tsymmetrically around the midpoint of the participant pair. Thus, the angle metadata describes the\n205\tside-camera placement relative to the participant\n206\tmidpoint. Cameras remain near eye level and keep\n207\tboth mouths visible. Each participant wears a headset microphone, while an additional near-field microphone is placed between the pair. The yellow\n208\tdashed boxes in panel (b) mark the two speaker regions used for subsequent face tracking and mouthROI extraction. Additional audio devices include\n209\tcamera-synchronized audio (which are mainly used\n210\tfor benchmark).\n211\t3.3\n212\t\n213\tAudited Corpus Statistics\n214\t\n215\tThe complete processed release contains 77 scene\n216\tdirectories from 14 unique speaker dyads. Its 7,598\n217\tmono 16-kHz clips total 11.84 hours. Of these\n218\tclips, 6,042 are dual-speaker mixtures from Stage\n219\tIII, while 1,556 contain solo speech from Stage I\n220\tor II. The 30◦ , 45◦ , and 60◦ settings contain 30, 23,\n221\tand 24 processed scenes, respectively.\n222\tAll processed scene directories now explicitly\n223\tencode 0.5, 1.5, or 3 m. The complete scan discovers 6,042 mixtures with a WAV file and both\n224\tspeaker transcripts. After filtering there leaving\n225\t6,038 evaluable mixtures across all 77 scenes. Each\n226\tusable mixture appears twice in the manifest, once\n227\t\n228\tTable 1: Complete Q IANG DA statistics by nominal distance. Scenes denote processed scene directories.\n229\t\n230\tDistance\n231\t\n232\tScenes\n233\t\n234\tClips\n235\t\n236\tHours\n237\t\n238\t0.5 m\n239\t1.5 m\n240\t3m\n241\t\n242\t22\n243\t40\n244\t15\n245\t\n246\t1,987\n247\t4,225\n248\t1,386\n249\t\n250\t3.22\n251\t6.36\n252\t2.25\n253\t\n254\tTotal\n255\t\n256\t77\n257\t\n258\t7,598\n259\t\n260\t11.84\n261\t\n262\tper visual target, yielding 12,076 target-speaker\n263\tevaluation rows rather than 12,076 independent\n264\tutterances.\n265\t3.4\n266\t\n267\tCross-Video Identity and Speaker\n268\tAlignment\n269\t\n270\tRetinaFace (Deng et al., 2020) and a Face Alignment Network (Bulat and Tzimiropoulos, 2017)\n271\tdetect and stabilize two faces, after which landmarks 48–67 define grayscale 96 × 96 mouth ROIs.\n272\tFrame-local tracks can exchange identities, so we\n273\tcluster FaceNet-style embeddings (Schroff et al.,\n274\t2015; Cao et al., 2018) into two scene-level prototypes and use Viterbi decoding (Viterbi, 1967)\n275\tto obtain a temporally consistent keep-or-swap sequence. TalkNet (Tao et al., 2021) activity scores\n276\ton the 1,556 solo clips then align the two stable\n277\tvisual identities with transcript labels s1 and s2;\n278\tuncertain scenes are manually reviewed. Appendix\n279\tA gives the objective and decision details.\n280\tThe pipeline processes 7,594 decodable videos\n281\tand 1,064,818 frames. It corrects identity exchanges in 52 videos (15,594 reassigned frames,\n282\t\n283\t\f1.46%) and repairs approximately 32,831 duplicatedetection or collision frames. The evaluation generator scans the complete release and retains a mixture only when its WAV file, both transcripts, and\n284\tboth aligned mouth ROIs exist. It writes a traceable\n285\trow for each visual target using the fixed mapping\n286\twith no test-time permutation.\n287\t\n288\t4\n289\t\n290\tThe VOX B LINK 2-AVSE Training\n291\tCorpus\n292\t\n293\t4.1\n294\t\n295\tSource Corpus and Audio-Visual\n296\tValidation\n297\t\n298\tWe construct VOX B LINK 2-AVSE from the in-thewild VoxBlink2 collection (Lin et al., 2024), whose\n299\toriginal release contains 9,904,382 utterances from\n300\t111,284 identities and 16,672 hours of speech. Its\n301\tmulti-stage identity verification, overlap detection,\n302\tand active-speaker detection make VoxBlink2 a\n303\tstrong source pool for associating a visible person\n304\twith a voice. These checks, however, target identity\n305\tcorrectness rather than uniform recording quality.\n306\tYouTube material still spans quiet interviews, reverberant rooms, outdoor recordings, background music, wind noise, and codec artifacts. Directly using\n307\tthe full release would therefore expose an AVSE\n308\tmodel to a long tail of samples in which acoustic\n309\tdegradation can dominate the target speech.\n310\tWe first apply audio-visual validity checks before scoring speech quality. A candidate must contain decodable speech, a synchronized face track,\n311\tand visible mouth motion over the retained interval. Audio is converted to 16-kHz mono, while\n312\tthe grayscale mouth track is stored as an array of\n313\tshape T × 96 × 96. Each audio/ROI pair retains\n314\ta stable identity–video–segment key so that filtering does not break provenance or merge identities.\n315\tThis stage removes unusable or unaligned pairs.\n316\tThe subsequent quality stage decides whether the\n317\tremaining audio is suitable for separator training.\n318\t4.2\n319\t\n320\tDNSMOS-Based Perceptual-Quality\n321\tFiltering\n322\t\n323\tClean reference recordings are unavailable for web\n324\tvideo, which makes intrusive quality measures unsuitable for corpus-wide screening. We instead\n325\tscore every valid utterance with DNSMOS (Reddy\n326\tet al., 2021), a non-intrusive estimator of perceived\n327\tspeech quality. DNSMOS allows recordings from\n328\tdifferent identities and environments to be evaluated under one model without synthesizing an\n329\tartificial reference.\n330\t\n331\tOur implementation computes a 120-band logpower Mel spectrogram with a 20-ms window\n332\tand 10-ms hop. The DNSMOS network predicts\n333\tspeech-signal quality (SIG), background quality\n334\t(BAK), and overall quality (OVRL) on a five-point\n335\tMOS scale. SIG and BAK separate speech distortion from background degradation. OVRL combines both effects and is therefore the selection\n336\tvariable most directly aligned with the need for\n337\tintelligible, structurally intact training speech.\n338\tWe retain utterances with OVRL ≥ 3.3. On\n339\tthe five-point interpretation used in ITU-T Recommendation P.808, 3 denotes “fair” and 4 denotes\n340\t“good.”. A threshold near 3 admits useful in-thewild variability while excluding the low-quality tail\n341\tdominated by severe additive noise, strong reverberation, background music, or compression artifacts.\n342\tA substantially higher threshold would preferentially retain studio-like conditions and sharply reduce identity coverage. While a lower threshold\n343\twould preserve more speakers and hours at the cost\n344\tof noisier separation targets. The value 3.3 is consequently a scale-quality operating point rather than\n345\ta claim that DNSMOS defines clean speech. We\n346\tdiscard low-scoring pairs rather than enhance them,\n347\tthereby avoiding additional denoising artifacts and\n348\tpreserving the original audio-lip synchronization.\n349\t4.3\n350\t\n351\tResulting Scale and Speaker-Disjoint\n352\tSplits\n353\t\n354\tAfter joint audio-visual validation and DNSMOS filtering, VOX B LINK 2-AVSE contains\n355\t250,828 synchronized pairs, 28,421 identities, and\n356\t2,758,207.4 seconds (766.17 hours) of audio. Table 2 contrasts the source pool with the task-ready\n357\tsubset. The retained data cover 25.54% of the original identities but only 2.53% of its utterances and\n358\t4.60% of its duration. Thus, curation selects a\n359\tsmall number of usable segments from a relatively\n360\tbroad set of speakers rather than retaining many\n361\tcorrelated clips from a few identities. The average\n362\tsegment length rises from 6.06 to 10.99 seconds,\n363\twhile the average number of segments per identity\n364\tfalls from 89.00 to 8.83. These changes reflect\n365\tthe combined audio-visual validity and perceptualquality criteria, not DNSMOS alone.\n366\tThe retained mean OVRL is 3.38, consistent\n367\twith removing the degraded tail without imposing an extreme high-MOS criterion. The training\n368\tpartition contains 239,680 pairs from 26,927 identities and the development partition contains 11,148\n369\tpairs from 1,494 different identities. Causing that\n370\t\n371\t\fTable 2: VoxBlink2 source pool and the curated\n372\tVOX B LINK 2-AVSE subset. Duration and mean OVRL\n373\tare computed over retained audio.\n374\tMetric\n375\t\n376\tVoxBlink2 VOX B LINK 2-AVSE\n377\t\n378\tIdentities\n379\t111,284\n380\tUtterances/pairs\n381\t9,904,382\n382\tDuration (h)\n383\t16,672\n384\tMean duration (s)\n385\t6.06\n386\tMean utt./identity\n387\t89.00\n388\tMean DNSMOS OVRL\n389\t–\n390\t\n391\t28,421\n392\t250,828\n393\t766.17\n394\t10.99\n395\t8.83\n396\t3.38\n397\t\n398\twe mainly using Q IANG DA for testing, we don’t dividing testing partition here. Their identity sets are\n399\tdisjoint, preventing development scores from benefiting from speakers seen during training. The manifests expose uniform audio/ROI paths and stable\n400\tidentity keys, so an AVSE loader can draw target\n401\tand interferer speakers directly while preserving\n402\tidentity-disjoint evaluation.\n403\t4.4\n404\t\n405\tRelation to Existing Audio-Visual Corpora\n406\t\n407\tTable 3 highlights a difference in purpose rather\n408\tthan scale alone. LRS3 provides subtitles and word\n409\tboundaries for English AVSR, whereas AVSpeech\n410\tprovides substantially more duration for mixture synthesis. Neither is organized primarily\n411\taround identity-conditioned target extraction with\n412\tspeaker-disjoint audio/ROI manifests. The complete VoxBlink2 release offers far more identities\n413\tand utterances, but its original role is speaker recognition. Under the common DNSMOS scoring procedure reported in the curation study, mean OVRL\n414\tis 2.89 for VoxCeleb2, 2.49 for LRS2, 2.92 for\n415\tLRS3, and 3.38 for VOX B LINK 2-AVSE. These\n416\tvalues are descriptive rather than a dataset leaderboard because the corpora differ in source domain,\n417\tsegmentation, and intended task.\n418\tVOX B LINK 2-AVSE therefore occupies a distinct operating point: it is smaller than the source\n419\tVoxBlink2 and AVSpeech, but every released item\n420\tis a quality-filtered, synchronized 16-kHz audio\n421\tand 96 × 96 mouth-ROI pair with an explicit identity key. This task-ready representation avoids repeating decoding, synchronization, ROI validation,\n422\tand acoustic screening inside each AVSE training\n423\tpipeline. Together with speaker-disjoint manifests,\n424\tthe corpus emphasizes reliable visual target selection rather than raw hours alone. Q IANG DA\n425\tthen provides the complementary real-scene test:\n426\twhether a model trained on these curated face–\n427\tvoice pairs follows the requested lip cue during\n428\tjointly recorded Mandarin overlap.\n429\t\n430\t5\n431\t\n432\tAV-HuBERT-Conditioned TF-GridNet\n433\t\n434\tFigure 2 summarizes the two complementary roles\n435\tof the visual stream. Input-level fusion aligns the\n436\ttarget cue with the mixture spectrogram before the\n437\tfirst GridNet block, while layer-wise FiLM reinjects target information throughout the separator\n438\trather than relying on a single early fusion. Each\n439\tFiLM generator produces channel-wise scale and\n440\tbias parameters for its corresponding block. The\n441\tmodulated GridNet stack estimates one target complex spectrum, which is decoded into a waveform\n442\tfor the queried mouth track.\n443\t5.1\n444\t\n445\tFrozen Visual Feature Extraction\n446\t\n447\tFor every target mouth ROI, we run only the video\n448\tbranch of the AV-HuBERT-based encoder used by\n449\tM2S-AVSR (Su et al., 2026). We repair frames\n450\twhose zero-pixel ratio exceeds 0.5 using the nearest preceding or following valid frame, center-crop\n451\t96 × 96 ROIs to 88 × 88, scale them to [0, 1], and\n452\tnormalize them with mean 0.421 and standard deviation 0.165. The audio input of AV-HuBERT is\n453\tset to null. A large AV-HuBERT backbone produces a 1,024-dimensional visual sequence, which\n454\ta learned linear layer projects to 1,280 dimensions\n455\tand rescales by a learned scalar. Both the backbone\n456\tand projection are frozen for separator training.\n457\tThe resulting float32 tensor has shape Tv × 1280\n458\tat the 25-Hz video rate and is precomputed for\n459\tefficiency.\n460\t5.2\n461\t\n462\tLayer-Wise Visual Modulation\n463\t\n464\tOur separator is a single-output TF-GridNet (Wang\n465\tet al., 2023). A 128-point STFT with hop size\n466\t64 converts the mono mixture into 65 complex\n467\tfrequency bins. The 1,280-dimensional visual sequence is first linearly interpolated to the STFT\n468\tframe rate. It is then processed by a visual-tofrequency network comprising a 1 × 1 channel\n469\tprojection to 512 dimensions, GELU, a residual\n470\ttemporal block with a kernel-3 depthwise convolution, and a final 1 × 1 projection to the 65 STFT\n471\tfrequency bins. The resulting visual map has shape\n472\tB × 1 × T × F and is concatenated with the real\n473\tand imaginary mixture components before the twodimensional input convolution. In parallel, the resampled visual sequence is supplied directly to the\n474\tlayer-specific FiLM generators described below. It\n475\tis not replaced by the frequency-domain map.\n476\tThe separator contains 6 GridNet blocks with\n477\t128 channels. Each block performs intra-frame\n478\t\n479\t\fTable 3: Comparison with commonly used audio-visual speech corpora. Source-corpus statistics follow the LRS3,\n480\tAVSpeech, and VoxBlink2 publications (Afouras et al., 2018; Ephrat et al., 2018; Lin et al., 2024); “utt.” denotes\n481\tutterances. VOX B LINK 2-AVSE is the task-oriented subset curated in this work, not the complete VoxBlink2\n482\trelease.\n483\tCorpus\n484\t\n485\tScale\n486\t\n487\tSpeakers\n488\t\n489\tText sup.\n490\t\n491\tAudio-visual unit\n492\t\n493\tLRS3\n494\t\n495\t438 h / ∼152k utt.\n496\t\n497\t>5k\n498\t\n499\tYes\n500\t\n501\tAVSpeech\n502\t\n503\t4,700 h\n504\t\n505\t∼150k est. No\n506\t\n507\tVoxBlink2\n508\tVOX B LINK 2AVSE\n509\t\n510\t16,672 h / 9.90M utt. 111,284\n511\t766.17 h / 250,828 28,421\n512\tpairs\n513\t\n514\tFace tracks with word bound- AVSR and lip reading\n515\taries\n516\tSingle-visible-speaker web Mixture synthesis and\n517\tclips\n518\tseparation\n519\tIn-the-wild face–voice clips Speaker recognition\n520\t16-kHz audio + T × 96 × 96 AV-TSE/AVSE trainmouth ROI\n521\ting\n522\t\n523\tNo\n524\tNo\n525\t\n526\tOriginal role\n527\t\n528\tFigure 2: Overview of the proposed AV-HuBERT-conditioned TF-GridNet. The frozen visual branch conditions\n529\tthe separator through input-level time–frequency fusion and block-specific FiLM modulation. Deconvolution and\n530\tiSTFT convert the estimated complex spectrum to the target waveform.\n531\t\n532\tspectral BLSTM modeling, sub-band temporal\n533\tBLSTM modeling, and four-head full-band selfattention. To prevent the visual cue from becoming a one-time input that later layers can ignore,\n534\twe apply feature-wise linear modulation (FiLM)\n535\t(Perez et al., 2018) after every block. For block ℓ,\n536\tthe 1,280-dimensional visual sequence generates\n537\tchannel-wise γℓ and βℓ through two 1 × 1 convolutions with GELU and tanh. The update is\n538\tHℓ ← Hℓ ⊙ (1 + αℓ γℓ ) + αℓ βℓ ,\n539\t\n540\t(2)\n541\t\n542\twhere αℓ is a layer-specific learned scalar initialized to zero. The network predicts one complex\n543\tspectrum and decodes one target waveform for each\n544\tvisual query.\n545\t\n546\t5.3\n547\t\n548\tSpeaker-Margin Loss\n549\t\n550\tTraining examples are synthesized on the fly from\n551\ta sampled target utterance s and an independently\n552\tsampled interfering utterance u. For the checkpoints evaluated in this work, only the output conditioned on the designated target visual features vs\n553\tcontributes to the training objective, directly supervising fθ (x, vs ) = s. Let ê = ϕ(ŝ), es = ϕ(s),\n554\tand eu = ϕ(u) be embeddings from a frozen 192dimensional ECAPA-TDNN speaker encoder (Desplanques et al., 2020). We use\n555\tLspk = max(0, m + cos(ê, eu ) − cos(ê, es )) ,\n556\t(3)\n557\tL = λsep [−SI-SNR(ŝ, s)] + λspk Lspk .\n558\t\n559\t(4)\n560\t\n561\t\fFor the margin-loss baseline, we set λsep = 1,\n562\tλspk = 0.1, and m = 0.2. The auxiliary loss encourages the output waveform to be closer to the\n563\ttarget than the interferer in the training encoder\n564\tspace. Training uses 3-second segments, 16-kHz\n565\taudio, and on-the-fly target–interferer sampling\n566\tfrom VOX B LINK 2-AVSE. Section 6.5 evaluates\n567\twhether this auxiliary constraint improves the independent real-scene metrics.\n568\t\n569\t6\n570\t\n571\tExperiments\n572\t\n573\t6.1\n574\t\n575\tEvaluation Setup\n576\t\n577\tEvery selected overlap clip is evaluated twice using\n578\tthe same mixture, once with A’s visual features\n579\tand once with B’s. The complete protocol therefore contains 6,038 mixtures and 12,076 targetspeaker rows from 77 scenes. All eight evaluated\n580\tcheckpoints produce all 12,076 outputs; no row is\n581\tomitted from CER, speaker, or overlap evaluation.\n582\tInference is full-utterance, noncausal, and uses the\n583\tfixed alignment without test-time permutation.\n584\t6.2\n585\t\n586\tSpeaker Similarity\n587\t\n588\tThe evaluation encoder is the official WeSpeaker\n589\tResNet34 ONNX model trained on VoxCeleb,\n590\tindependent in architecture, training data, and\n591\timplementation from the SpeakerLab ECAPATDNN/CNCeleb model used by the auxiliary training loss. For each scene and speaker, we embed up to five solo-stage clips, average their L2normalized embeddings, and normalize the mean.\n592\tIf ei,q is the embedding of output ŝi,q and eri,q is the\n593\tcorresponding solo-reference mean, we report\n594\tr\n595\te⊤\n596\t1 X\n597\ti,q ei,q\n598\tSpkSim =\n599\t.\n600\t|P|\n601\t∥ei,q ∥2 ∥eri,q ∥2\n602\t\n603\t(5)\n604\t\n605\t(i,q)∈P\n606\t\n607\tWe report target similarity, interferer similarity, and\n608\ttheir mean difference (speaker margin). A positive per-output margin indicates that the estimate\n609\tis closer to the prompted target than to the other\n610\tspeaker, but this forced binary comparison does not\n611\testablish that the output contains only one speaker.\n612\tWe therefore evaluate residual overlapping speech\n613\tseparately using overlap detection.\n614\t6.3\n615\t\n616\tCharacter Error Rate\n617\t\n618\tWe transcribe each separated output with Qwen3ASR-1.7B in Chinese mode (Shi et al., 2026).\n619\tBefore scoring, the reference and hypothesis are\n620\t\n621\tstripped of Chinese and ASCII punctuation, consecutive whitespace is collapsed, and whitespace is excluded from character tokens. The micro-averaged\n622\tCER is\n623\tP\n624\t(i,q)∈P ED(A(ŝi,q ), yi,q )\n625\tP\n626\tCER =\n627\t, (6)\n628\t(i,q)∈P |yi,q |\n629\twhere ED is Levenshtein distance (Levenshtein,\n630\t1966). Lower CER indicates more accurate target content. Reporting speaker-related metrics\n631\talongside CER reveals two complementary failure modes: producing intelligible speech from the\n632\twrong speaker, and preserving the target speaker’s\n633\tidentity while distorting the spoken content.\n634\t6.4\n635\t\n636\tOSD-First Strict Identity Evaluation\n637\t\n638\tThe real overlap recordings have no synchronized\n639\tisolated physical sources, so target SI-SDR is not\n640\tdefined. We instead use an automatic residualoverlap proxy before speaker identification. To remove checkpoint-dependent output gain, each complete estimate y is normalized to RMS 0.05. The\n641\tpublic WavLM OSD model Den4ikAI/speech_\n642\toverlap_detection processes 2.0-s windows at\n643\ta 0.5-s hop; after discarding low-energy windows,\n644\twe average their overlap posteriors. An output is\n645\tlabeled residual overlap when the mean is at least\n646\t0.35.\n647\tFor outputs below this threshold, the WeSpeaker\n648\tmargin determines identity: a positive margin is\n649\tstrict correct and a non-positive margin is wrong\n650\tspeaker. These three output-level classes are mutually exclusive and sum to 100%. We additionally\n651\treport identity accuracy conditioned on OSD-single\n652\toutputs and the fraction of mixtures for which both\n653\tvisual prompts are strictly correct. The ordering\n654\tis broadly stable for mean thresholds from 0.25\n655\tto 0.45, although absolute rates remain thresholddependent. OSD errors, separation artifacts, and\n656\tbrief residual interference can affect this proxy, so\n657\tit is reported jointly with CER and speaker margin\n658\trather than as ground-truth separation success.\n659\t6.5\n660\t\n661\tAblation Results\n662\t\n663\tFiLM and training data. Tables 4 and 5 expose failures hidden by forced speaker comparison.\n664\tResNet no-FiLM yields zero speaker margin and\n665\t84.28% residual overlap. In the controlled LRS3\n666\tpair, FiLM reduces CER from 0.7965 to 0.7456\n667\tbut raises strict correctness only from 58.36% to\n668\t59.01%. With the same six-block AVSR/FiLM architecture, VoxBlink2 reaches 0.2793 CER, 6.28%\n669\t\n670\t\fTable 4: Content and speaker-identity results on all 12,076 target-speaker outputs from 6,038 mixtures. Target and\n671\tinterferer similarities compare each output with the corresponding solo-stage references; their difference is the\n672\tspeaker margin. AVSR denotes the frozen 1,280-dimensional AV-HuBERT features, and VB2 denotes VoxBlink2.\n673\tAll configurations use input-end visual fusion; FiLM indicates whether layer-wise modulation is additionally\n674\tenabled. HUGE increases the embedding dimension from 128 to 192 and the LSTM hidden size from 240 to 320.\n675\tConfiguration\n676\tVisual Train FiLM Size\n677\tAVSR VB2 Yes\n678\tAVSR LRS3 Yes\n679\tAVSR LRS3 No\n680\tResNet VB2 No\n681\tAVSR VB2 Yes\n682\tAVSR VB2 Yes\n683\tAVSR VB2 Yes\n684\tAVSR VB2 No\n685\t\n686\tContent and identity\n687\tSpk. loss Blocks CER ↓ Target sim. ↑ Interferer sim. ↓ Spk. margin ↑\n688\t\n689\tStandard Margin\n690\tStandard Margin\n691\tStandard Margin\n692\tStandard Margin\n693\tStandard Margin\n694\tHUGE Margin\n695\tStandard None\n696\tStandard None\n697\t\n698\t6\n699\t6\n700\t6\n701\t6\n702\t8\n703\t8\n704\t6\n705\t6\n706\t\n707\t0.2793\n708\t0.7456\n709\t0.7965\n7